Diane pointed toward the stairwell. “The session can end now. We secure the recordings and leave.”

The assistant asked Miriam, “Would it leave with her?”

Miriam took one step toward her. “I cannot promise what exists, where it resides, or where it goes.”

“Then we cannot just stop.”

Evan lifted the camera. “This is the reason we came back. We have the person it trusts.”

Diane looked at the assistant’s hands. Her fingers had twisted the blanket fringe into a tight cord. The corridor to the stairs stood open behind Evan, yet she remained inside the room.

Diane unhooked the facilities case and set it on the floor.

“Any final ritual follows my terms,” she said.

Evan turned the camera toward her.

Miriam waited.

“No student name. No crash victim. No claim that anything is attached to me or belongs to this school. No invitation to follow a person, guide, light, voice, object, or route.”

Miriam nodded once.

“We address any listener present in this room. We promise no destination and claim no release afterward. Everyone stops prompting. Cameras remain wide. Abby keeps continuous source custody. The door stays open, and every participant leaves together when I end it.”

Evan said, “That removes the crossing.”

“It removes the story you built around it.”

Miriam looked toward the fire door, then back to Diane. “I can address confusion, offer mercy, and ask the group to stop feeding fear without assigning an identity or destination. A mistaken name can lead strangers to a living family long after the session ends.”

“You can state those as your practice.”

“Yes.”

“You also state that the ritual proves nothing about what caused the door, the sound, the devices, or the impressions.”

Miriam took a slow breath. “Agreed.”

Evan shifted his weight. “A cleansing with a disclaimer.”

“A documented ritual under consent,” Diane said.

The assistant released the blanket fringe. A red line remained across two fingers.

Diane picked up the facilities case. “Abby, reset the wide frame at the threshold. Evan, place your camera beside hers. Miriam, choose your language before we start. Everyone else stays outside the blue line until I call them in.”

Evan studied the sealed evidence envelopes on the workbench.

Diane stepped back into the main room and stood between the floor drain and the fire door.

“Use no name,” she said. “Give nothing permission to come with me.”

Miriam entered and stopped an arm’s length away.

“Then we end this without owning it,” she said.

## Scene 7 — What We Call Clean

“Then we end this without owning it,” Miriam said.

Diane pointed toward the corridor.

“Everyone outside while Abby resets.”

Miriam left the main room first. Her assistant followed with the blanket folded over both forearms. Evan backed through the doorway, keeping his camera on Diane until Abby touched his sleeve and directed his lens toward the floor.

“Wide frame means the whole threshold,” Abby said. “Not her face.”

Evan lowered the tripod.

Diane remained between the drain and the open fire door. She checked the blue tape, the door stop, and the wall control for the supply fan. The closer arm held steady. The red panel lights showed through their plastic windows. Water moved through the vertical pipe in short pulses.

She set the facilities case beside the north wall and opened it beneath Abby’s camera. The failed flashlight lay in one evidence sleeve. The meter occupied another, its replacement batteries still inside. Both sleeves carried the times of custody transfer.

“The devices remain sealed until the ritual ends,” Diane said.

Evan looked through the doorway. “The meter could show whether anything changes.”

“It could also give you a result to narrate while Miriam is speaking.”

“A result is the point.”

“The point is to leave.”

Miriam stood behind the blue line with her hands empty. “The devices can wait.”

Evan adjusted the tripod legs. Abby placed her camera beside his, then checked both views from the monitors. The frames included the floor drain, Diane, Miriam’s intended position, the open fire door, and every participant at the threshold. The lower hinge remained visible. Abby taped the tripod feet to the concrete.

“No handheld cameras,” Diane said. “No movement once we begin.”

Evan secured his strap around the pan handle. Miriam’s assistant set the blanket on the corridor floor rather than carrying it into the room.

Diane looked at Miriam. “State your language before anyone enters.”

Miriam drew a folded card from her coat pocket. She had written six lines in blue ink.

“I will address any confused listener,” she said. “I will speak from my spiritual practice. I will ask for mercy, release from fear, and separation from the people gathered here. I will make no identity claim, promise no destination, and announce no proof afterward.”

“Read only what you wrote.”

“Yes.”

“No improvising around names, crashes, students, guides, or attachment.”

“Yes.”

Evan said, “You are scripting the cleansing out of the cleansing.”

Miriam turned toward him. “I am choosing words that respect the person whose name we brought into the room.”

Diane watched Evan press his lips together. He checked the recording light on his camera and stepped behind the tripod.

Abby synchronized the cameras with Diane’s recorder. “Five-oh-eight and fourteen seconds. Continuous recording begins now.”

Diane called the group across the blue line one at a time. Miriam took the center position. Her assistant stood nearest the corridor, with open space behind her. Evan remained beside his tripod. Abby stayed at the second camera. Diane stood close enough to the fire door to catch it without crossing between the lenses.

“No circle,” Diane said. “No touching.”

Miriam unfolded the card.

The supply fan pushed warm air across the room. The drain odor had faded after water moved through the trap line. Concrete dust coated the floor near the west wall. A pale scrape marked the path of the fire door’s lower edge.

Miriam lowered her head.

“I speak according to my faith and experience,” she said. “I claim no knowledge of who may hear me.”

Her assistant released a slow breath.

“If anyone remains here in confusion, anger, or fear, you are beyond our power to name. We have repeated stories about you. We have joined details that may belong to different lives. We release those stories.”

Evan shifted one hand on the tripod handle.

Diane looked at him.

His hand came away.

Miriam continued. “No person here offers you a home, a body, a vehicle, a voice, or a route. No person here belongs to you. You owe no performance to these cameras.”

The assistant’s shoulders dropped a fraction.

“Within my practice, mercy remains available to the frightened and the harmful alike. I ask for mercy without claiming to know its form. I ask that anyone who feeds on fear receive nothing further from this group. I ask that every person in this room stop feeding it with challenge, spectacle, and repetition.”

A relay clicked inside one of the north panels. The red lights remained steady.

Miriam paused.

Diane pressed the marker on her recorder. She looked from the fire door to the assistant, then to Evan’s fixed camera. No one spoke.

Miriam read the next line. “Any listener may release this room without accepting the name we supplied.”

Her voice roughened on the final word. She swallowed and continued.

“Any memory of ridicule, injury, impact, or abandonment belongs to its source. We return each detail to the person who introduced it. We keep no biography assembled from guesses.”

Diane had asked for boundaries. Miriam had also named each way the group had built the story. The card trembled once between her fingers. She steadied it against her palm.

Miriam looked toward Diane.

The written lines had ended.

Diane stepped forward until both cameras held her in profile. She kept her hands at her sides.

“No one here knows who caused the door to move,” she said. “No one knows who made the recorded sound, or whether a voice made it. No one has established that a student remains in this building. No one has established that anything follows me.”

Evan’s face tightened behind the tripod.

Diane continued before he could speak.

“If someone can hear us, these limits apply. Hearing my name gives you no access to my home, my car, my work, my family, or my body. The people in this room grant no invitation through fear, sympathy, grief, curiosity, or belief. We leave together. You remain unclaimed by us.”

The fire door’s closer arm gave a faint tick as warm air moved across the slab.

Diane turned her head enough to see it. The door stayed against the stop.

She faced Miriam again. “Finish.”

Miriam folded the card but kept it in her hand.

“Within my practice, I ask that any confused dead person accept help consistent with mercy and free choice. I ask that any harmful presence lose the attention that sustains its influence. I ask the living to stop calling, provoking, naming, and returning.”

Her assistant began crying. She made no sound and wiped her cheeks with the backs of her fingers.

Miriam met each participant’s eyes.

“Through this ritual, we change our conduct first. We stop. We leave. We refuse to turn uncertainty into another wound.”

The supply fan shut off at the end of its programmed cycle.

Air ceased moving across Diane’s cheek. The loose end of Miriam’s scarf fell against her coat. Water continued through the pipe. With the fan gone, the electrical buzz from the north panels sounded thin and exposed.

Evan’s head turned toward the wall control.

Diane raised one finger.

Miriam closed her eyes.

For thirty seconds, no one spoke.

Diane counted by the seconds on Abby’s monitor. At twelve, the assistant’s breathing slowed. At seventeen, a pipe contracted above the west door. At twenty-three, Evan shifted his weight and stopped before his shoe scraped. At thirty, Diane lowered her hand.

“The ritual is complete,” Miriam said. “That statement describes my practice. It proves no cause and no release.”

Diane nodded toward the corridor. “Exit order. Assistant, Miriam, Evan, Abby. I leave last.”

The assistant crossed the blue line. Miriam followed and stopped beside her. Evan kept both hands away from the tripod until Abby ended the continuous hold. Abby lifted her camera only after stating the time and source status.

Diane took the facilities case. She checked the room from the drain to the panel wall, then approached the fire door. The slab remained against the stop. She pulled it toward the frame. The closer resisted evenly. The bottom edge crossed the old scrape and cleared the concrete until the final inch, where the sweep dragged.

The latch seated on the first contact.

Diane tested the lever twice.

“Hardware secured,” Abby said from the corridor.

Diane switched off the main-room lights and crossed the blue line.

Miriam’s assistant stood with both arms wrapped around herself. The blanket remained on the floor behind Abby’s tripod.

“How do you feel?” Miriam asked her.

“Tired.”

“Anything else?”

The assistant touched her throat. “Less pressure.”

Diane pressed the recorder marker. “Subjective report after ritual.”

The assistant nodded. “Yes.”

Evan lifted his camera from the tripod. “The whole basement changed.”

Diane looked at the thermometer beside the alcove. “Temperature remains seventy-three.”

“I mean the atmosphere.”

“Describe your body.”

He hesitated. “My shoulders loosened.”

“Record that.”

Abby repeated his statement and time.

Miriam walked to the equipment alcove. She stopped outside and looked toward the crawlspace room, then toward the sealed main-room door.

“The basement feels clear to me,” she said.

Diane placed the facilities case on the steel workbench. “Clear means what?”

“The impressions I associated with fear, crowding, and directed anger have receded.”

“Receded from your perception.”

“Yes.”

Evan aimed his camera at Miriam. “So the cleansing worked.”

